1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,880
Myth or reality: a heat pump stops working once it's below freezing outside.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:05,513
Myth.

3
00:00:05,633 --> 00:00:07,213
Cold air still holds heat.

4
00:00:07,333 --> 00:00:13,195
True zero heat is absolute zero, minus four hundred fifty-nine point six seven degrees.

5
00:00:13,315 --> 00:00:20,210
Measured from there, thirty-two-degree air has about ninety-three percent of the heat energy of seventy-degree air.

6
00:00:20,330 --> 00:00:23,037
Zero-degree air, about eighty-seven percent.

7
00:00:23,157 --> 00:00:25,015
A heat pump doesn't make that heat.

8
00:00:25,135 --> 00:00:28,857
It moves it inside, using electricity to run a compressor.

9
00:00:28,977 --> 00:00:38,130
ENERGY STAR cold-climate models must still deliver at least one point seven five units of heat per unit of electricity at five degrees.

10
00:00:38,250 --> 00:00:39,839
An electric heater gives one.

11
00:00:39,959 --> 00:00:42,834
So it works harder in the cold, but it keeps working.

12
00:00:42,954 --> 00:00:45,342
Which is why the real question isn't freezing…
